!! Overview

Puts the fields of the //kms// suite in the tiddler editor. The fields themselves — what each one means, the values it allows and what they mean — are defined by [[KMS Ontology|https://github.com/nikorion/TW-KMS-Ontology]]: see its readme and its //Fields// tab. This plugin decides how each one is edited:

{{$:/plugins/nikorion/base-fields/readme/controls}}

The controls sit on rows of their own, each right next to the core row it neighbours: ''Project'', ''Epistemology'' and ''Disciplines'' share the row right above the tags box, ''Technology'' and ''Equipment'' the row right after it, and ''Role'', ''Maturity'', ''Lifecycle'', ''Needs'' and ''Status'' the one under the type control. A field the ontology gains later gets a control too, with nothing to change here: a vocabulary field at the end of the type row, a free field at the end of the row under the tags. No core tiddler is overridden and no core row is touched.

A row packs its blocks to the left at their natural width rather than spreading them out, so that a block's own parts stay together — the delete button sits next to the control it clears rather than at the far end of a stretched row. Too narrow to hold them all, a row wraps.

A label gives the field's description as its tooltip. The list fields (Technology, Equipment, Disciplines) carry no visible label: their placeholder says what they are for, which spares the width.

Each list field's picker is strictly independent of the others and of the wiki's tags: the Technology dropdown offers only the values already used in `technology`, never `equipment`'s or the tags', and so on. Pills are drawn like tag pills, per-value colour included, but fall back to a colour of their own field rather than the palette's tag colour, so pills from different fields stay told apart.

A field only shows where the ontology says it applies: ''Status'', for instance, only on tasks, task lists and routines, or on a tiddler with no role. A field that does not apply but still holds a value (say `needs` after the role changed to `task`) is shown all the same, framed in red, and its tooltip names the role and says to clear the field (//The tasks do not use this field — clear it//).

Each control carries the same clear button the core puts on Type, always visible. An empty Role shows a greyed hint in place of a value. Maturity, Lifecycle and Status are radio buttons, each with a //blank value// (Stub, Active, None) that stands for the empty field: it is the one checked while the field is empty, and choosing it clears the field.

The fields are hidden from the list of fields at the bottom of the editor, through the core's own `$:/config/EditTemplateFields/Visibility/` mechanism: editing them twice on one form would be one place too many. Setting the corresponding tiddler to `show` brings any of them back.

[[Dynamic Table|https://github.com/nikorion/TW-Dynamic-Table]] edits its list-field columns with the same picker as this editor.

!! Requirements

TiddlyWiki >= 5.3.0 and [[KMS Ontology|https://github.com/nikorion/TW-KMS-Ontology]].

